<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<assembly xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v1" manifestVersion="1.0">
  <!--
    processorArchitecture is not optional, whatever the documentation implies.
    Without it Windows rejects the whole manifest and refuses to start the
    binary, reporting "Invalid Xml syntax" on the assemblyIdentity line - which
    is valid XML, and sends you looking in the wrong place. Found by bisecting
    this file against the event log, not by reading it.
  -->
  <assemblyIdentity version="1.0.0.0" processorArchitecture="amd64" name="AkuWM" type="win32" />

  <!--
    THE DEVELOPMENT MANIFEST. Identical to app.manifest except that uiAccess is
    off, so a build can be run straight from its output directory instead of
    being signed and installed into Program Files first. Chords over a game do
    not work with it - that is what the other one is for.

    uiAccess is what lets AkuWM's keyboard hook see the keys typed into a
    window running at a higher integrity level than it does - which is what
    every game is. Measured, not assumed: with it off, an elevated window was
    in front for 14 of 30 seconds, was typed into, and not one keystroke
    reached the hook (spike S1).

    It is not elevation. The process still runs as the user (asInvoker); it is
    only allowed to drive the user interface of higher-integrity windows, the
    accessibility case this flag exists for. Windows grants it only to a binary
    that is Authenticode-signed with a trusted certificate AND sits in a
    directory non-administrators cannot write to - tools/install-uiaccess.ps1
    arranges both.

    A build with this manifest will not start from anywhere else. Publish with
    -p:UiAccess=false to get one that will, for the development loop.

    What AkuWM does with what the hook sees: docs/input-and-anticheat.md.
  -->
  <trustInfo xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v2">
    <security>
      <requestedPrivileges xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
        <requestedExecutionLevel level="asInvoker" uiAccess="false" />
      </requestedPrivileges>
    </security>
  </trustInfo>

  <!-- Windows 10 and 11. Without it the OS reports itself as Windows 8. -->
  <compatibility xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:compatibility.v1">
    <application>
      <supportedOS Id="{8e0f7a12-bfb3-4fe8-b9a5-48fd50a15a9a}" />
    </application>
  </compatibility>

  <!--
    Per-monitor v2: without it Windows virtualises every coordinate on the
    150 % monitor, and a layout computed from those numbers puts windows in the
    wrong place at the wrong size. Set in code as well, so it holds however the
    binary is published.
  -->
  <application xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
    <windowsSettings>
      <dpiAwareness xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2016/WindowsSettings">PerMonitorV2</dpiAwareness>
      <longPathAware xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2016/WindowsSettings">true</longPathAware>
    </windowsSettings>
  </application>
</assembly>
